<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ricciarda Malaspina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a>–<a href="/years/1553">1553</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ricciarda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a> (1497–1553) was Marchioness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, heiress of the Malaspina lords of Massa. Her marriage in 1520 to Lorenzo Cybo, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>, brought the state to the Cybo family, and she governed it herself for long periods. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberico-i-cybo-malaspina">Alberico I Cybo-Malaspina</a> was made Prince of Massa in 1568.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ricciarda was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a>, the daughter of Antonio Alberico II Malaspina, marquis of Massa and lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, and Lucrezia d'Este. As he had no sons, her father named his eldest daughter Eleonora as heiress, in violation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>. Eleonora married Scipione Fieschi, count of Lavagna, but died in 1515, and the next year Fieschi married Ricciarda. He died four years later without a son, leaving her one daughter, Isabella Fieschi. Her father's revised will made her heiress and usufructuary on behalf of her future sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After her father's death in 1519 Ricciarda kept control of his states despite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>. Amid the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the Italian states she won the favour of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and in 1529 an unusual imperial decree invested her with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in her own right, allowing her to pass them to daughters as well as sons. Her husband Lorenzo Cybo had himself named co-owner by the emperor the following year. The couple lived apart for more than a decade; after Lorenzo tried to oust her by force in 1538, she had his grant revoked in 1541.</p><p>In 1546 her eldest son Giulio Cybo took the lordship from her by arms, threats and a purchase contract he could not pay, and she resumed power on 27 June 1547. Giulio was implicated in a pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conspiracy, arrested at Pontremoli on 22 January 1548 and beheaded for treason in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 18 May; her plea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> for pardon failed. Lorenzo died on 14 March 1549.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ricciarda ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> until her death in 1553 and was succeeded by her younger son Alberico, held to be legitimate but most probably the son of her brother-in-law and long-time lover, Cardinal Innocenzo Cybo. Her will required her heirs to bear the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaspina">Malaspina</a> beside Cybo, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cybo-malaspina">House of Cybo-Malaspina</a>, which held Massa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> until 1790.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ricciarda Malaspina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the marriage of <b>Ricciarda Malaspina</b>, the state passed to the Cybo family, and Alberico I Cybo-Malaspina was made prince in 1568; it became a duchy in 1664.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-massa-and-carrara">Duchy of Massa and Carrara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Massa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Bagni di Lucca, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.03,10.13&amp;year=1533" aria-label="Massa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ricciarda Malaspina"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5620 3784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5620 3784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.03,10.13&amp;year=1533">What was here in 1533? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
